with q as (
  select $astroComet_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "A newly discovered comet has an orbital period of about 900,000 years and a retrograde orbit inclined 130 degrees to the ecliptic. Which reservoir did it come from, and what in its orbit tells you?", "options": ["The scattered disc, because its long period marks it as a body that Neptune flung inward and Jupiter now controls.", "The Oort cloud, because a spherical shell has no preferred plane, so it delivers comets isotropically (retrograde as often as prograde) on gigantic, near-parabolic orbits with periods from about 200 years up to millions.", "The scattered disc, because retrograde motion is the signature of a flattened disc processed by the giant planets.", "The main asteroid belt, because only a belt-native body could survive on such a long-period orbit."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The chapter's central move is that a comet's orbit is a return address you can read. The Oort cloud is a roughly spherical shell at the edge of the Sun's gravitational reach, so it has no preferred plane and delivers comets from every direction, retrograde as often as prograde, with isotropic inclinations. Its enormous size means gigantic semi-major axes and therefore periods running from the conventional 200-year cut up to millions of years, on near-parabolic orbits. A 130-degree (retrograde), high-inclination orbit essentially cannot come from the flattened scattered disc, which is pre-sorted into low inclinations by Neptune's in-plane encounters and delivers low-inclination, prograde, short-period Jupiter-family comets instead. Inclination alone nearly settles the question because the two reservoirs have different shapes: flattened disc versus spherical shell. The long period only reinforces the Oort-cloud reading; the main belt is not a comet reservoir of this kind at all."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Comet nuclei are made largely of ice, yet 67P's nucleus reflects only about 6 percent of the light hitting it (albedo near 0.06), darker than coal. What resolves this apparent paradox?", "options": ["The nucleus is bright ice, but the coma of gas and dust blocks most of the light before it can reflect off the surface.", "The ice is dyed dark by carbon monoxide fluorescence, which absorbs the incoming sunlight.", "As the comet warms, the near-surface volatile ices sublimate and escape, leaving behind a lag deposit of dark, carbon-rich, radiation-processed dust; the ice does the leaving and the soot stays behind to do the absorbing, so the surface is a cinder even though the interior is icy.", "Comet nuclei are actually made of solid rock, not ice, which is why they are as dark as asphalt."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The surface is not ice, even though the bulk is ice-rich. As the nucleus is warmed near the Sun, the volatiles closest to the surface sublimate away and escape, leaving a lag deposit of involatile dust and organic, radiation-processed material, which is dark and carbon-rich. The ice does the leaving; the soot stays behind and does the absorbing, giving 67P a geometric albedo near 0.06 (Halley's was blacker still, near 0.04). A comet looks bright in our sky not because its surface is bright but because it is shedding a huge, sunlit cloud of gas and dust. The dark crust also explains patchy activity: sunlight absorbed by the crust conducts down to buried ice and vents through cracks as collimated jets. The other options are wrong: it is not a solid rock, CO+ fluorescence colors the ion tail rather than dyeing the nucleus, and the darkness is a property of the surface itself, not the coma blocking light."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "A well-developed comet shows two tails pointing in different directions. Which statement correctly pairs each tail with its driving force, direction, and colour?", "options": ["The ion tail is charged gas dragged almost exactly anti-sunward by the solar wind and glows blue from CO+ fluorescence; the dust tail is neutral grains nudged by radiation pressure that curve and lag along the orbit and shine yellowish-white by reflecting sunlight.", "The ion tail is neutral dust pushed by radiation pressure and curves along the orbit; the dust tail is charged gas swept straight back by the solar wind.", "Both tails are driven by the solar wind, but the blue ion tail points anti-sunward while the yellow dust tail points toward the Sun.", "The ion tail curves because ions retain their orbital velocity, while the dust tail is straight because radiation pressure blows grains directly away from the Sun."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "This is the chapter's showcase: one object, one instant, two streamers governed by two unrelated forces. The ion (plasma) tail forms when ultraviolet sunlight ionizes coma molecules (carbon monoxide especially, whose CO+ fluoresces blue); once charged, the ions are seized by the solar wind blowing radially outward at about 400 km/s and swept almost exactly anti-sunward, so the ion tail is straight, blue, and points away from the Sun regardless of the comet's motion, twitching when the wind gusts. The dust tail is neutral grains that the solar wind cannot grip; they are nudged by radiation pressure (sunlight's momentum), a far gentler push, and crucially they retain the orbital velocity they had at release, so each grain coasts on its own Keplerian orbit and the ensemble spreads into a broad, curved sheet lagging behind along the path. It shines yellowish-white because it reflects the full solar spectrum. The distractors swap the mechanisms, colors, or directions, or wrongly point the dust tail sunward."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Rosetta measured 67P's bulk density at 533 kg/m3, while solid water ice is about 920 kg/m3 and the comet's ice-plus-dust solids would average roughly 1500-2000 kg/m3. What is the main conclusion, and how does it connect to the comet's shape?", "options": ["The comet is pure water ice with a small amount of dust, which explains why its density is just below that of solid ice.", "The comet must be mostly water with dissolved gases, since 533 kg/m3 is close to the density of liquid water.", "The low density means the comet's interior has been compressed by repeated high-speed impacts into a dense rocky core surrounded by fluffy ice.", "The nucleus is highly porous, on the order of 75 percent empty space, and you preserve fluff like that only through gentle, low-velocity accretion, the same soft-assembly story told by its bilobed contact-binary shape."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "A bulk density of 533 kg/m3 is roughly half that of solid water ice and far below the 1500-2000 kg/m3 of the actual ice-plus-dust ingredients, so most of the comet is simply not there: the nucleus is highly porous, on the order of 75 percent empty space, with voids on every scale from microscopic pores to metre-sized cavities. It is closer to a drifting snowbank or loosely stuck dust bunnies than to solid rock. This porosity is itself a formation clue: you do not compress fluffy aggregates to this low a density by slamming them together, but you do preserve fluff if the building blocks settled gently and were never crushed. That is the same soft-accretion story told by 67P's bilobed 'rubber duck' shape, a contact binary of two cometesimals that merged at a few metres per second at most, with a delicate neck and preserved internal layering, echoing Arrokoth. The distractors misread the low density as near-water composition or invent a compressed rocky core, contradicting the porosity."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Earth's oceans have a D/H ratio of about 1.56 x 10^-4, 67P's water is about 5.3 x 10^-4, and carbonaceous chondrites sit around 1.4 x 10^-4. What does the D/H evidence conclude about the source of Earth's water?", "options": ["The D/H ratio is too easily altered after water forms to be a reliable fingerprint, so it cannot settle the question either way.", "Comet water is about three times too deuterium-rich to be Earth's water, so a mass balance limits comets to at most a few percent of the oceans; the terrestrial value instead matches carbonaceous chondrites, handing the argument to the asteroids.", "67P's ratio matches Earth's within measurement error, confirming the old story that comets delivered the oceans.", "Because 67P is a single Jupiter-family comet, its high ratio is an outlier and most comets actually match Earth's oceans."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The D/H ratio is a fingerprint of where water formed: colder, more distant, less-processed environments lock in more deuterium, and the ratio is very hard to alter after the fact, so water carries its birth signature across the age of the solar system. Match it and you match the source; miss it and you rule the source out. 67P's water at 5.3 x 10^-4 is about 3.4 times the terrestrial VSMOW value of 1.56 x 10^-4, and 67P is not an outlier: measured comets, Oort-cloud and now this Jupiter-family one, cluster high, generally 1.5 to 3 times Earth's. A simple mass balance, 1.56 = f(5.3) + (1-f)(1.4), gives f about 0.04, so at most a few percent of Earth's water can be cometary. The measurement does not just negate comets; it hands the argument to the carbonaceous chondrites, whose D/H around 1.4-1.5 x 10^-4 sits right on the terrestrial value, pointing to the icier bodies of the outer asteroid belt as the source of the oceans."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Rosetta detected molecular oxygen (O2) in 67P's coma. Explain why this detection was such a surprise, what specific feature of the measurement showed the O2 was primordial rather than a later by-product, and what it reveals about the conditions in which comets formed. Connect this to the broader claim that a comet is 'a sample of the primordial outer solar system kept in a freezer.'", "rubric": "A strong answer explains that the ROSINA mass spectrometer detected O2 at about 4 percent relative to water, and that no one had predicted it because molecular oxygen is chemically reactive and should not survive 4.5 billion years frozen next to all the other reactive material in a comet. The decisive feature of the measurement is that the O2 abundance tracked the water abundance as the comet's activity varied: the two came out together, from the same icy material, which means the O2 was incorporated into the water ice as it originally formed rather than being produced later by a surface reaction. This forces the conclusion that the O2 was frozen in at very low temperature, early, before the material was gathered into the comet, and has sat undisturbed ever since, making the detection a thermometer pointed at the comet's birth that reads cold, primordial, and undisturbed for the age of the solar system. The best answers tie this to the framing that both reservoirs (scattered disc and Oort cloud) are cold storage at a few tens of kelvin for 4.5 billion years, so a comet is not a recently formed object but a preserved sample of the primordial outer solar system briefly warmed in front of us; the O2, a molecule that 'should not be there,' turns out to be one of the strongest constraints on how and where comets formed. Credit may also mention the co-detected prebiotic ingredients (glycine, its precursor methylamine, and phosphorus) as further evidence that the cold outer disk's chemistry is preserved intact, though the O2 argument is the core."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter deliberately 'breaks the taxonomy' of comet versus asteroid. Explain, using active asteroids and dormant/extinct comets as evidence, why the categories dissolve at the edges. Distinguish the two axes involved, give at least one concrete example of each population, and state the chapter's thesis about what the labels actually track.", "rubric": "A strong answer identifies the two independent axes the chapter separates: where an object comes from (its reservoir/provenance, which is permanent) versus what it is doing right now (its activity, a time-dependent phase). These axes do not line up cleanly, which is why the comet/asteroid labels, which track behaviour rather than a bright line in nature, fail at the boundary. On the active-asteroid side, the answer should note objects on ordinary main-belt orbits (dynamically native asteroids) that nevertheless grow comae or tails, and that a tail has several possible causes that must be diagnosed: ice sublimation (e.g., 133P/Elst-Pizarro, a main-belt comet whose activity recurs near perihelion, evidence ice survives in the outer belt), rotational shedding driven mechanically by the YORP spin-up, and impacts (e.g., P/2010 A2, whose tail was collision debris with an X-shaped structure). The key point: a tail means 'this object is losing mass right now,' not 'comet.' On the other side, dormant and extinct comets: every perihelion builds the dark lag crust until it either seals in the remaining ice (dormant, can reactivate) or the volatiles are exhausted (extinct), leaving a dark, inert, kilometre-scale lump indistinguishable from an asteroid, identifiable only by a giveaway orbit (a high Tisserand parameter). A good concrete example is 3200 Phaethon, which looks like an asteroid with no persistent tail yet is the parent of the Geminid meteor shower and sheds dust at its close perihelion, sitting astride the boundary. The thesis: the universe did not draw the line we drew; it made a continuum of icy-to-rocky bodies across a range of orbits, and our two words work fine at the centre of each category but fail at the edges because the taxonomy tracks behaviour, and behaviour is a phase an object passes through, not a fundamental kind it belongs to. Excellent answers may add the coda that meteor showers are comet dust streams (Perseids from Swift-Tuttle, Orionids and eta Aquariids from Halley) and that the Geminids, from asteroid-like Phaethon, are the exception proving the debris streams ignore the boundary too."}]$astroComet_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/comets', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
